ary barriers reduce risk but do not replace supervision. Notify the team about occupants with mobility, respiratory, hearing, or other needs that may affect safe coordination.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">4</span><div><h3>Utilities and Temporary Interruptions</h3><p>Plumbing, electrical, HVAC, internet, or appliance service may be temporarily interrupted. Ask in advance which systems may be affected and plan around critical needs. Do not restore a disconnected utility without authorization. Temporary shutdowns are often necessary to complete work safely and verify the installation.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">5</span><div><h3>Dust, Noise, and Daily Cleanup</h3><p>Construction dust can travel through doorways, return-air paths, and foot traffic. Reasonable containment and cleanup practices are used according to the scope, but no occupied renovation is completely dust-free. Close doors, protect sensitive electronics, and discuss special concerns before demolition begins. Daily cleanup makes the site safer; final cleaning occurs near completion.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">6</span><div><h3>Neighbors and Property Rules</h3><p>Inform the team about homeowner-association restrictions, gate access, shared driveways, noise limits, elevator reservations, or neighbor agreements. Customers are generally responsible for obtaining approvals assigned to the property owner unless the contract states otherwise. Early communication prevents avoidable interruptions.</p></div></div><h2>Customer Action Checklist</h2><ul class="sc-checklist"><li>Read all project documents and attachments before approval.</li><li>Keep contact information and authorized decision-makers current.</li><li>Use the client portal and support tickets for tracked communication.</li><li>Respond promptly to selections, access requests, and approvals.</li><li>Keep copies of signed documents, payments, receip