            'content' => '<div class="sc-cover"><span class="sc-kicker">Smart Choice Customer Guide</span><h2>Managing Changes and Unforeseen Conditions</h2><p>A clear explanation of concealed conditions, added work, change documentation, price effects, and schedule impacts.</p><blockquote>Professional information designed to help you make confident decisions before, during, and after your project.</blockquote></div><div class="sc-grid"><div class="sc-card"><h3>What this guide covers</h3><p>Planning, responsibilities, communication, documentation, safety, and the practical steps that support a successful customer experience.</p></div><div class="sc-card"><h3>How to use it</h3><p>Read the complete guide, keep it with your project records, and return to the relevant section whenever a decision or question arises.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">1</span><div><h3>Why Projects Change</h3><p>Existing buildings can contain concealed deterioration, moisture damage, noncompliant prior work, structural conflicts, inaccessible utilities, or conditions that cannot be confirmed before demolition. Customers may also request upgrades or scope additions after work begins. A change process keeps these decisions organized.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">2</span><div><h3>Documenting the Condition</h3><p>When a new condition is found, the team may use photographs, measurements, inspection findings, supplier information, or professional recommendations to explain it. Work may pause in the affected area until the required direction is approved. This protects the project from covering a problem without a responsible solution.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">3</span><div><h3>Pricing Additional Work</h3><p>Additional price can include labor, materials, equipment, permits, engineering, disposal, supervision, delays, remobilization, or work already completed to protect the site. The cost should be reviewed together with the revised scope. An estimate for a concealed condition may still require adjustment if the full extent is not visible.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">4</span><div><h3>Schedule Effects</h3><p>A change can affect more than the immediate task. It may alter inspections, product orders, trade sequencing, access, curing time, or completion dates. The team should communicate material schedule effects, but exact timing may depend on approvals and supplier availability.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">5</span><div><h3>Customer-Requested Changes</h3><p>Requests made directly to field personnel are not automatically authorized. Submit changes through the approved channel so scope, price, materials, and timing can be reviewed. Proceeding without documentation creates confusion about responsibility and payment.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">6</span><div><h3>Protecting the Project Record</h3><p>Keep approved change orders, revised drawings, photographs, invoices, and messages with the original contract. A complete record helps everyone understand why the final scope, price, or schedule differs from the initial plan.</p></div></div><h2>Customer Action Checklist</h2><ul class="sc-checklist"><li>Read all project documents and attachments before approval.</li><li>Keep contact information and authorized decision-makers current.</li><li>Use the client portal and support tickets for tracked communication.</li><li>Respond promptly to selections, access requests, and approvals.</li><li>Keep copies of signed documents, payments, receipts, and closeout records.</li><li>Report safety concerns or unexpected conditions as soon as they are discovered.</li></ul><div class="sc-success"><strong>Smart Choice Standard:</strong> Done Right Through Professional Service. Our objective is to combine skilled construction coordination with clear records, respectful communication, and dependable customer support.</div>',
        ];
        $guides[] = [
            'name' => 'Project Scheduling and Inspections',
            'slug' => 'smart-choice-scheduling-inspections',
            'title' => 'Understanding Construction Schedules and Required Inspections',
            'description' => 'How schedules are built, why dates move, how inspections affect progress, and what customers can do to prevent delays.',
            'content' => '<div class="sc-cover"><span class="sc-kicker">Smart Choice Customer Guide</span><h2>Understanding Construction Schedules and Required Inspections</h2><p>How schedules are built, why dates move, how inspections affect progress, and what customers can do to prevent delays.</p><blockquote>Professional information designed to help you make confident decisions before, during, and after your project.</blockquote></div><div class="sc-grid"><div class="sc-card"><h3>What this guide covers</h3><p>Planning, responsibilities, communication, documentation, safety, and the practical steps that support a successful customer experience.</p></div><div class="sc-card"><h3>How to use it</h3><p>Read the complete guide, keep it with your project records, and return to the relevant section whenever a decision or question arises.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">1</span><div><h3>A Schedule Is a Coordinated Sequence</h3><p>Construction scheduling connects labor, materials, access, inspections, curing periods, and customer decisions. Many tasks cannot begin until the prior stage is complete. A date shown early in the project may be a planning target rather than an unconditional guarantee.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">2</span><div><h3>Common Schedule Variables</h3><p>Permitting, inspection availability, weather, concealed conditions, supplier delays, custom products, change orders, utility coordination, and trade dependencies can change the sequence. The team should communicate meaningful changes, but daily construction activity may vary as conditions develop.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">3</span><div><h3>Customer Decisions</h3><p>Late selections, unavailable products, missed access, or delayed approvals can stop dependent work. Respond promptly to requests and confirm changes in writing. When an owner-supplied item is required, verify that it is complete, undamaged, compatible, and on site before the scheduled installation.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">4</span><div><h3>Inspections</h3><p>Required inspections verify specific stages of permitted work. The inspector may approve, reject, request clarification, or require correction. Work that must remain visible for inspection cannot always be covered merely because the next trade is available. Inspection results can affect the following schedule.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">5</span><div><h3>Weather and Exterior Work</h3><p>Rain, wind, temperature, humidity, and saturated surfaces can affect demolition, concrete, masonry, roofing, stucco, paint, waterproofing, and material delivery. Rescheduling weather-sensitive work protects safety and product performance.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">6</span><div><h3>Completion Forecasts</h3><p>As the project advances and unknowns are resolved, the completion forecast becomes more accurate. Customers should plan furniture delivery, move-in, events, or other commitments with reasonable flexibility until final inspections and punch-list work are complete.</p></div></div><h2>Customer Action Checklist</h2><ul class="sc-checklist"><li>Read all project documents and attachments before approval.</li><li>Keep contact information and authorized decision-makers current.</li><li>Use the client portal and support tickets for tracked communication.</li><li>Respond promptly to selections, access requests, and approvals.</li><li>Keep copies of signed documents, payments, receipts, and closeout records.</li><li>Report safety concerns or unexpected conditions as soon as they are discovered.</li></ul><div class="sc-success"><strong>Smart Choice Standard:</strong> Done Right Through Professional Service. Our objective is to combine skilled construction coordination with clear records, respectful communication, and dependable customer support.</div>',
        ];
        $guides[] = [
            'name' => 'Quality Control, Punch Lists and Closeout',
            'slug' => 'smart-choice-quality-closeout',
            'title' => 'From Quality Checks to Final Project Closeout',
            'description' => 'How work is reviewed, how punch lists are handled, and what records customers should receive and retain.',
            'content' => '<div class="sc-cover"><span class="sc-kicker">Smart Choice Customer Guide</span><h2>From Quality Checks to Final Project Closeout</h2><p>How work is reviewed, how punch lists are handled, and what records customers should receive and retain.</p><blockquote>Professional information designed to help you make confident decisions before, during, and after your project.</blockquote></div><div class="sc-grid"><div class="sc-card"><h3>What this guide covers</h3><p>Planning, responsibilities, communication, documentation, safety, and the practical steps that support a successful customer experience.</p></div><div class="sc-card"><h3>How to use it</h3><p>Read the complete guide, keep it with your project records, and return to the relevant section whenever a decision or question arises.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">1</span><div><h3>Quality Is Built in Stages</h3><p>Many important quality elements are concealed by the final finish. Framing, reinforcement, fastening, waterproofing, rough plumbing, wiring, insulation, substrate preparation, and manufacturer requirements must be checked before they are covered. Photographs and inspections may document these stages.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">2</span><div><h3>Normal Construction Variations</h3><p>Hand-installed materials and existing structures can have reasonable variation. Existing walls may not be perfectly square, materials may contain natural shade or pattern differences, and repairs may not make the entire surrounding surface new. The contract and accepted trade standards determine the required result.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">3</span><div><h3>Creating a Punch List</h3><p>Near substantial completion, identify visible items that appear incomplete or require correction. Describe each item clearly and avoid duplicating the same concern through multiple channels. A punch list is for completion and correction within the agreed scope, not for adding new work without authorization.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">4</span><div><h3>Access for Corrections</h3><p>Provide reasonable access for scheduled punch-list work. Some corrections require drying, curing, supplier parts, or coordination with a specialty trade. The team should communicate the expected sequence and document completion.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">5</span><div><h3>Final Documents</h3><p>Closeout records may include executed agreements, approved changes, invoices, receipts, inspection results, product information, maintenance instructions, warranties, photos, and lien-related documents when applicable. Save them in a secure location and download portal copies before account access changes.</p></div></div><div class="sc-step"><span class="sc-step-number">6</span><div><h3>Care and Maintenance</h3><p>New construction and remodeling materials require routine care. Follow manufacturer cleaning and maintenance instructions, maintain sealants and drainage, control indoor humidity, and report unusual conditions early. Damage caused by misuse, deferred maintenance, movement, or unrelated conditions is different from an installation defect.</p></div></div><h2>Customer Action Checklist</h2><ul class="sc-checklist"><li>Read all project documents and attachments before approval.</li><li>Keep contact information and authorized decision-makers current.</li><li>Use the client portal and support tickets for tracked communication.</li><li>Respond promptly to selections, access requests, and approvals.</li><li>Keep copies of signed documents, payments, receipts, and closeout records.</li><li>Report safety concerns or unexpected conditions as soon as they are discovered.</li></ul><div class="sc-success"><strong>Smart Choice Standard:</strong> Done Right Through Professional Service. Our objective is to combine skilled construction coordination with clear records, respectful communication, and dependable customer support.</div>',
